WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.700
EDHRec is a fascinating tool that has grown
to monolithic stature, eclipsing most other

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.320
resources in the EDH ecosystem.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.480
And this is not for no reason—a neatly organized,
ordered list of what cards other people play

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.179
is a useful tool.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:18.971
Other people put thought and effort into crafting
decks, and you get a summary of that thought

00:00:18.971 --> 00:00:22.310
and effort delivered directly to your eyes
via a snappy UI.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:27.490
However, it’s not a perfect tool, due to
a variety of factors.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.710
In the first Magic: the Gathering video I
posted to this channel, I made a point to

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:34.890
discuss some downsides of EDHRec that I hadn’t
seen mentioned before, and while it’s a

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.900
cool video that you should definitely go watch,
the contents of that video aren’t what I

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.440
want to talk about today.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.840
Instead, I want to talk about a point brought
up in the comments to that video, that being

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.949
that displaying cards by percent inclusion
in decks flattens a lot of information about

00:00:48.949 --> 00:00:50.700
the decks they’re played in.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.680
If there are three cards each played in a
third of decks, it’s difficult to tell whether

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.879
they’re all played in the same third of
decks, if they’re all played in different

00:00:57.879 --> 00:01:00.359
decks, or something in between.

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:04.089
And this is a flaw pretty inherent to the
construction of the website—it’s not some

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:07.439
emergent problem that popped up and is awaiting
a solution.

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:11.490
But it got me thinking—is there anything
that users of the website can do to adapt

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.880
to this fact?

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.840
When I look at a card on EDHRec, can I figure
out what it’s doing there, what other cards

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.290
go with it, and what all that says about the
decks it's played in, or even about the players

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.330
that built those decks?

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:30.010
The answer to this question is… complicated,
but as a process it’s taken me down a series

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:34.500
of rabbit holes as useful as they are bizarre,
and I want to share that journey with you

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.590
today.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:39.350
As a fairly straightforward example, in late
December I was looking to make an aggressive

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.770
partner commanders deck, and I was intrigued
by the Commander pairing of Malcolm, Keen-Eyed

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.210
Navigator, and the freshly printed Francisco,
Fowl Marauder.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.590
The appeal for me was strong—two low cost
partners, one of which ramps and the other

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:56.570
of which filters, which can be paired with
a further slate of low-cost pirates to enable

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.210
all sorts of shenanigans.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:05.010
EDHRec had a mere 310 decks for this freshly-minted
pairing, so a question we can ask is “who

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.679
else jumped on these two as a worthwhile commander
pair?”

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:12.530
Looking at the 10 high synergy cards—the
ones disproportionately run in this commander

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:17.290
pair compared to the color as a whole—offers
a clear picture.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:22.840
Almost 60% of Francisco-Malcolm decks run
Thassa’s Oracle and Demonic Consultation,

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.940
an instant win combo requiring a mere two
cards and three mana.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.739
Looking further, we can see a bunch more high-powered
staples also at around 60% usage, but scroll

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:38.300
down a bit beyond that and we can see a very
different type of card at approximately 35%

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.470
usage—a couple of four mana pirates with
abilities useful in slower, more midrange-y

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.470
decks.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.720
Ramirez and Hostage Taker are both fun cards,
but they’re decidedly not mana well spent

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.790
in a cEDH Thoracle deck.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:56.510
For this reason, I’m going to call these
two cards, along with Thoracle, “indicator

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.930
cards”—cards that give you a rough idea
on the upper bound of players playing a particular

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.790
type of deck.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:08.170
If I use the indicator cards of Thoracle and
Ramirez, I can start to construct a Venn diagram

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:14.660
with a combo circle containing 58% of decks,
and a midrange circle containing 36% of decks.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:19.780
I wouldn’t expect a ton of overlap, and
indeed, a search with EDHRec’s filter feature

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.670
plus some quick math tells me that there’s
about 7% of decks that run both cards, and

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.959
about 13% of decks that run neither card.

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:30.830
Now what does this sort of analysis tell me?

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:35.269
Well my first takeaway from seeing the 58%
Thoracle play rate was, god damn, I guess

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:38.849
I’m on the right track if I’m looking
to make a high-powered deck.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:42.569
To be clear, Thassa’s Oracle plus Demonic
Consultation will be run in some percentage

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:45.360
of any deck with blue and black in it, because
people will try to make high-powered versions

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.500
of any old commander, but 58% is a lot of
decks.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.900
While it’s not a wincon I’d go for, the
fact that such a high percentage of people

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:57.100
building Francisco-Malcolm run Thoracle communicates
to me, somebody who’s building on a budget,

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.030
that I’m probably getting a lot of bang
for my 84 cents from the command zone.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:04.730
For my part, I ended up including none of
the indicator cards I mentioned in the deck

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.720
I ended up building, because I’m a unique
and special boy.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.040
For another example, about a year ago I was
building a Skeleton Ship deck.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.639
It was intended to be a glacially slow proliferate
deck, and it had all the fixins’—the parallax

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:23.180
enchantments, some impressively slow wincons,
and the original storage lands that enter

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.150
tapped and don’t tap for non-stored mana.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:30.220
The commander was a card that could be played
as a mediocre removal engine, but most games

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:36.510
it just sits there as a meme, a perfect encapsulation
of the slow mass of nothing I was doing.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:40.000
So what decks do other Skeleton Ship players
build?

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.830
Looking at the high synergy cards, the top
5 are all cards that pair with Skeleton ship’s

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.770
ability, so already other people are deviating
from my gameplan by actually caring about

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.180
the commander.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.940
Next up, in roughly 50% of decks are a bunch
of proliferate cards, which seems sensible

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.160
enough to me—that’s what I’m doing.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.920
After that, in about 30% of decks, are a bunch
of skeletons, and in about 15% of decks are

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.620
a handful of questionable but undeniably skeleton-themed
cards.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:07.620
Excellent.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:12.870
Okay, so these are gonna be the three archetypes,
with our indicator cards for each being Freed

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.949
from the Real, Thrummingbird, and Reassembling
Skeleton.

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:21.699
We can now make a triple Venn diagram, and
fill it out by simply plugging in every combination

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:24.970
of these three cards in EDHRec’s filter
tool.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:29.660
And upon doing this, the first thing I noticed
was that cares-about-commander-ability and

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:34.990
cares-about-counters seem almost like one
very diverse super-category, whereas Skeletons

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:36.910
were definitely more distinct.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:41.210
If you’re a freak like me you’re probably
also wondering—what is that other 15%?

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:46.840
I did another filter excluding these indicator
cards plus an additional one from each category

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.980
to account for slight variances in card choice,
and the result is—pirates and cards with

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:53.560
ships on them.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.360
Respect, honestly.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.569
The Skeleton Ship page was fascinating for
the opposite reason as the Francisco-Malcolm

00:05:59.569 --> 00:06:00.569
one.

00:06:00.569 --> 00:06:04.710
This EDHRec page looks like something of a
tour of low-power EDH.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.790
There’s the players who are earnestly building
around the Glorious card known as Skeleton

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:12.970
Ship, the players who took it as a jumping
off point for some strange quest into the

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:17.889
unknown, the people who are here for a commander
for their janky skeletons decks, and the people

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:21.810
who appreciate the card for all of its skeleton
and ship goodness.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:26.389
I picked out Skeleton Ship as the commander
for my bad meme deck, and it looks like I’m

00:06:26.389 --> 00:06:31.060
in good company with that fact, but the interesting
thing is how many different types of meme

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.710
are present here.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:38.229
And honestly, the process of making this video
and returning to the glorious EDHRec page

00:06:38.229 --> 00:06:42.210
has only increased my amount of love for all
my bone homies out there, living their best

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.360
Skeleton Ship lives.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.350
For our third example, let’s talk about
Archelos, Lagoon Mystic.

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:52.539
This page has a lot more decks than the previous
two I mentioned, and yet somehow it’s honestly

00:06:52.539 --> 00:06:55.639
the most confusing EDHRec page I’ve ever
been on.

00:06:55.639 --> 00:06:59.910
High up on the page, we get some stuff that
makes sense—things that tap and untap the

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.009
commander, and lands-matter cards.

00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:05.810
Sure, that makes sense, cards that lead to
lands entering tapped are good when the commander

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.639
has them come in untapped.

00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:11.580
We also get Villainous Wealth—okay yeah,
I guess if you’re playing a lot of lands,

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.389
you may as well have an appropriately-colored
outlet.

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:18.610
And then you get down to stuff like King Macar,
the Gold-Cursed.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:21.860
Are people playing him because Archelos has
him enter tapped and thus lets you get an

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:23.870
Inspire trigger a turn early?

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:28.389
Maybe, but there’s also a lot of tap/untap
synergies sprinkled throughout the page.

00:07:28.389 --> 00:07:31.770
Not enough for me to get a confident idea
of what people are doing, but certainly a

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:35.760
handful, seemingly present in a hair over
20% of decks.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.319
Next up is tribal turtle decks, and for this
we can see a bunch of turtles played in roughly

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:45.819
15% of decks, with about half that many decks
also running a bunch of questionable turtle-themed

00:07:45.819 --> 00:07:46.819
cards.

00:07:46.819 --> 00:07:50.630
We’ll call these turtle players, and dedicated
turtle players.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:53.360
Finally, let’s talk gates.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.470
The indicator cards I’m going to use here
are Baldur’s Gate, Nine-Fingers Keene, and

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:01.650
Maze’s End, cards played in a decent portion
of decks that you’d probably only want in

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:02.789
a gates deck.

00:08:02.789 --> 00:08:07.710
Now, these play-rates confuse me a little
because, in my opinion as a professional gates

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:11.880
enjoyer, Baldur’s Gate is a much worse card
than these other two.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.849
For early-game purposes it’s a wastes in
a three color deck, and the limitation to

00:08:15.849 --> 00:08:20.539
three colors means that even if you run every
single in-color gate, only a third of your

00:08:20.539 --> 00:08:21.870
lands will be gates.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:25.990
I don’t love Keene either—its triggers
will whiff more than a quarter of the time

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:30.060
in a three color deck—but at least it’s
a decently statted creature.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:34.909
Maze’s End is my personal favorite of these
in an Archelos deck—it comes in untapped

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:39.080
and can find you an untapped gate immediately,
and can be paired nicely with extra land play

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.240
effects.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.790
And that’s not even the strongest way to
use Maze’s End in an Archelos deck.

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:49.720
I recently posted a video to my Patreon discussing
what ended up being my highest-effort deck,

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:53.339
and you can stick around to the end of this
video for a preview of that.

00:08:53.339 --> 00:08:58.160
On some level, these adventures are cool simply
because I enjoy them—I enjoy a good puzzle,

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.310
I enjoy putting myself in somebody else’s
brain and asking why they decided to run a

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.130
particular card.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:08.470
In theory EDHRec has an archetypes feature
that gives breakdowns on different deck types,

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:12.660
but it’s not very good—wow, you’re telling
me there are Francisco-Malcolm decks that

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.579
involve pirates, treasure, and sacrificing?

00:09:15.579 --> 00:09:18.280
That’s crazy I never would have guessed.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.500
So, in absence of a feature like this, I enjoy
the challenge of snooping around and figuring

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:23.740
things out.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.580
However, this is also the part of the video
where I tell you that this goofy little thing

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:32.130
I like to do while using EDHRec is actually
fantastic exercise that will prevent all sorts

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:33.550
of mistakes down the line.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:38.450
One of the biggest pitfalls I’ve seen EDHRec
users fall into is where they add cards that

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:42.839
look good without thinking about what deck
those cards belong in or how many of a single

00:09:42.839 --> 00:09:45.000
card type belong in a deck.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.130
This results in decks which have some amount
of synergy in theory, but which in practice

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:55.610
feel disjointed and clunky, containing a frankenstein-like
mishmash of components from various decks.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:59.770
For this reason, I’d advocate spending an
extra moment trying to parse what specific

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:03.230
types of deck are actually being built with
the different cards.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:06.680
Even just keeping in mind that there are different
decks doing different things within a given

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:10.010
commander will likely be a great help to newer
players.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:14.740
EDHREC is a good tool, but it’s not one
you can go on autopilot with.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:18.780
To echo the conclusion of the EDHRec effect,
you should try to keep in mind where the cards

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:23.660
you’re looking at are coming from, and that,
no matter how snappy the UI is, the stuff

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:27.570
you’re seeing isn’t magical, it’s just
data slurry.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:31.100
With that said, thanks for watching, and here’s
a preview of a bonus video I made about the

00:10:31.100 --> 00:11:09.079
Archelos deck I ended up building.

